<div>
<h2 id="what-happened">Instapaper Now Ships Pre-Installed on BOOX Palma 2 and Palma 2 Pro</h2>

<p>On August 12, 2026, Good e-Reader reported that <strong>Instapaper now comes pre-installed on BOOX Palma 2 and Palma 2 Pro</strong> devices. Open the app from the home screen, sign in with your Instapaper account, and your saved articles — including highlights and notes — sync straight to the E Ink screen. For anyone who has spent years juggling a read-it-later service and an e-reader, that is a small announcement with a big consequence: the most popular read-later app on the web now runs natively on the most popular Android E Ink pocket reader, with no export step, no email gateway, and no subscription required.</p>

<p>The app that ships on the Palma 2 and Palma 2 Pro supports the full set of core Instapaper features: syncing your articles with your Instapaper account, saving new articles to that account from the device, liking, archiving, and deleting items, reading offline, and accessing your highlights and notes. In other words, the pre-installed app is not a stripped-down companion — it is Instapaper itself, tuned for a one-handed E Ink screen.</p>

<h2 id="why-e-ink">Why E Ink Is the Natural Home for Instapaper</h2>

<p>Instapaper was built for one thing: reading long-form web articles without the distractions of the open web. E Ink readers were built for the same thing, which is why the combination has felt inevitable for years. The BOOX Palma series makes the match especially obvious. The Palma is a pocket-sized, phone-shaped E Ink reader with a color display, an adjustable warm-to-cool front light, a fingerprint sensor, and full Android with Google Play support. It is designed to be held in one hand and read for hours — exactly the use case Instapaper was created for back in 2008, when Marco Arment launched it as a read-it-later service for the iPhone.</p>

<p>The current Palma 2 pairs a 6.13-inch color E Ink (Kaleido 3) display with an Android 13-based system and the Google Play Store pre-installed, at around $249. The Palma 2 Pro adds stylus support and an optional cellular variant, and this summer BOOX also debuted the even smaller 3.97-inch Picco for readers who want something truly pocket-sized. Because the entire Palma line runs Android, it was always possible to install Instapaper from Google Play manually — the change in August 2026 is that it now arrives out of the box. For BOOX, bundling Instapaper is a statement about what the Palma is for: not a note-taking tablet, not a comics device, but a serious reading machine for the article web. Our <a href="/blog/boox-palma-epub-reading-2026/">BOOX Palma EPUB reading guide</a> covers the device family in depth.</p>

<h2 id="what-you-get">What the Pre-Installed Instapaper App Can Do</h2>

<p>The announcement lists the features that matter most for daily reading:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Account sync</strong> — sign in once and your entire Instapaper queue, including items saved from your phone or browser, appears on the Palma.</li>
  <li><strong>Saving from the device</strong> — you can add articles to your Instapaper account from the Palma itself, which is handy when you find something worth reading while browsing on the device.</li>
  <li><strong>Full queue management</strong> — like, archive, and delete articles the same way you do in the iOS or Android apps, and the changes sync back.</li>
  <li><strong>Offline reading</strong> — articles are stored on the device, so you can read on a plane, a train, or anywhere without Wi-Fi.</li>
  <li><strong>Highlights and notes</strong> — your highlights and margin notes from other devices are available on the Palma, and new ones you make on E Ink sync back to the rest of your setup.</li>
</ul>

<p>Instapaper's optional Premium subscription adds extras such as full-text search across your entire archive, unlimited notes, and text-to-speech — features that are especially pleasant on E Ink, where the slow screen refresh makes everything else feel meditative. But none of the core reading workflow requires Premium, and the free tier is fully usable on the Palma.</p>

<p>This is also a good moment to remember where Instapaper came from. After launching in 2008, the service was acquired by Pinterest in 2016 and then spun back out as an independent company in 2018. It has outlived every competing read-it-later service of its era. In late July 2026 it shipped Instapaper 10, the biggest iOS update in over a decade, with a Liquid Glass design overhaul and Home Screen widgets — the same update our <a href="/blog/instapaper-epub-export-2026/">Instapaper to EPUB guide</a> covers from the export side. The BOOX bundling is the Android complement to that momentum.</p>

<h2 id="vs-kindle">Instapaper on BOOX vs. the Kindle Workflow</h2>

<p>For years, the standard way to read web articles on an e-reader was to send them to a Kindle. That pipeline has been getting less appealing. In late January 2026, Android Central, Android Authority, and Pocket-lint all reported that one of the Kindle's most popular free features — pushing web articles to your device — was moving behind a paid subscription tier priced around $60 a year. Reports of the same period noted that the change directly hit Instapaper users who had relied on the free Kindle delivery route. No single outlet has confirmed the exact terms, but the direction was clear: the free article-to-Kindle bridge was closing.</p>

<p>The native Instapaper app on BOOX sidesteps that entire problem, because there is no bridge to pay for. The app talks to your Instapaper account directly, on a device that is not owned by a store. Here is how the main workflows compare:</p>

<table>
