    <td>Export, convert, transfer</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<p>Kobo readers got a similar native integration back in August 2025, when Instapaper arrived on Kobo devices; the BOOX bundling brings the same idea to Android E Ink hardware. Our <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kindle vs. Kobo vs. PocketBook comparison</a> explains why that matters for the broader e-reader market.</p>

<h2 id="setup">How to Set Up Instapaper on Your Palma</h2>

<p>If you already have a Palma 2 or Palma 2 Pro with the August 2026 firmware, setup takes about two minutes:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Open the Instapaper app</strong> from the home screen. It will be in the app drawer or on the first page of pre-installed apps.</li>
  <li><strong>Sign in</strong> with your Instapaper account, or create a free account on the login screen. Your saved articles, folders, highlights, and notes sync automatically.</li>
  <li><strong>Save articles from your other devices</strong> using the Instapaper browser extension, the iOS or Android share sheet, or the email-to-account address. They appear on the Palma within seconds of syncing.</li>
  <li><strong>Pull down to refresh</strong> the queue when you open the app, then tap any article to read it on the E Ink screen. Adjust the warm-to-cool front light to match the room.</li>
  <li><strong>Highlight and note as you read</strong> — long-press to select text, then highlight or add a note. Everything syncs back to your other devices.</li>
</ol>

<p>If you want to read Instapaper content on a device that does not have a native app — an older Kindle, a PocketBook, or a desktop e-reader — the export route still works exactly as before. Instapaper can export individual articles or entire folders as EPUB files, which you can then convert and transfer with <a href="/blog/calibre-cli-batch-conversion/">Calibre</a>. The native BOOX app does not replace that workflow; it simply makes it unnecessary on the device where you read the most.</p>

<h2 id="epub-still-matters">When You Still Want EPUB</h2>

<p>It is worth being clear about the EPUB angle, since this site lives and breathes the format. The native Instapaper app on BOOX is a reading experience, not a file format. Your articles live in Instapaper's cloud, not as files you own. That is great for convenience — everything is synced, everywhere, instantly — but it is not a backup strategy.</p>

<p>For archiving, exporting to EPUB remains the right move. Instapaper's export creates a clean, DRM-free EPUB of any article or folder, which you can keep in Calibre, convert to other formats, or move to any e-reader you own. If Amazon ever shuts down your access to a device, or if a service changes its terms, your EPUB archive is the copy that survives. Our <a href="/blog/instapaper-epub-export-2026/">Instapaper to EPUB guide</a> walks through the three main export methods: the native export, Calibre automation for batch jobs, and browser extensions for one-click downloads. The same reasoning applies to the rest of your reading: if you are <a href="/blog/migrate-kindle-library-to-epub/">migrating a Kindle library to EPUB</a>, keep your own copies of the files.</p>

<h2 id="which-boox">Which BOOX Device Should You Use?</h2>

<p>If the pre-installed Instapaper app tempts you toward the BOOX ecosystem, the choice comes down to form factor:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Palma 2</strong> — the standard pocket reader. Color E Ink, fingerprint sensor, ~$249. Best balance of price and everyday usability for article reading.</li>
  <li><strong>Palma 2 Pro</strong> — adds stylus support and an optional cellular variant. Choose it if you want to annotate articles with a pen or read on the go without tethering.</li>
  <li><strong>Picco</strong> — BOOX's new 3.97-inch Tile-line reader for readers who want the smallest possible device. Still runs Android, so Instapaper installs from Google Play even though the pre-install announcement currently covers the Palma 2 family. Our <a href="/blog/onyx-boox-picco-pocket-epub-reader-2026/">Picco coverage</a> has the details.</li>
  <li><strong>Larger BOOX tablets</strong> — Note Air and Tab models run the same Android software, so Instapaper works there too, with more screen space for two-page article layouts.</li>
</ul>

<p>For most people, the Palma 2 is the sweet spot: it fits in a pocket, reads for days on a charge, and now opens straight into your Instapaper queue. If you already read EPUBs on a Palma, the new app is simply the best read-it-later layer that device has ever had.</p>

